Answer: Ans: Operations such as addition, subtraction, multiplication and division are related jobs. Writing a separate independent program for each of them would mean fifteen programs for fifteen operations, or fifty programs for fifty operations, and every program would repeat the same structure. With methods, each operation is written once as a method inside a single program, and it is called whenever it is needed. A method does one job, can be called many times with different values, and keeps the program organised. This is a common interview question on methods. Q2. Write a Java program in which addition, subtraction, multiplication and division are separate methods. Ans: Each operation is a method of the Calculator class that takes two numbers and returns the result. The main method calls each method with the values 20 and 10 and prints the result. The division method converts the first number to double so that the result keeps its decimal part.

```java
public class Calculator {
static int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
static int subtract(int a, int b) {
return a - b;
}
static int multiply(int a, int b) {
return a * b;
}
static double divide(int a, int b) {
return (double) a / b;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("Addition: " + add(20, 10));
System.out.println("Subtraction: " + subtract(20, 10));
System.out.println("Multiplication: " + multiply(20, 10));
System.out.println("Division: " + divide(20, 10));
}
}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output: Addition: 30 Subtraction: 10 Multiplication: 200 Division: 2.0 Adding another operation only needs one more method in the same class, and not a new program.
